<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Maipú</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1818">1818</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chilean-war-of-independence">Chilean War of Independence</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Maipú was fought on 5 April 1818 near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago">Santiago</a>, Chile. The patriot army under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-de-san-martin">José de San Martín</a> decisively defeated the royalist forces of Mariano Osorio, a few weeks after Chile had proclaimed its independence. The victory secured Chilean independence and allowed San Martín to prepare his expedition to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-maipu.jpg" alt="Battle of Maipú" width="320" height="223" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Johann Moritz Rugendas · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:BatallaDeMaipu.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After a surprise defeat at Cancha Rayada in March, San Martín and O'Higgins destroyed the main royalist army at the <b>Battle of Maipú</b> on 5 April 1818, securing central Chile.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-chile">Republic of Chile · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Royalist forces regained control in 1814, but José de San Martín and Bernardo O'Higgins won at Chacabuco in 1817 and <b>Maipú</b> in 1818, when Chile declared independence as the Republic of Chile.</blockquote><a href="/state/captaincy-general-of-chile">Captaincy General of Chile</a></li></ul></section>
